"# Task 26: Object Types, Density & Variety Analysis\n**Status:** Complete initial classification  \n**Date:** 2026-09-28  \n**Scope:** 64 objects + 14 support materials (assets, documents, datasets, places, practices)  \n**Chapters:** F1.5–F1.13 and F1.13 Networks\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Object Inventory by Material Type\n\n### Ceramics & Pottery (13 objects)\n- **Vessels for daily/household use:** Jar with cover (Jun ware, WS-061), bowl with waterfowl (Goryeo, WS-063), footed bowl (Kashan, WS-021), vase with inlay (Goryeo, WS-062), black-figure amphoriskos (WS-093), janiform flask (WS-094), ennion cup (WS-095), bowl with maker's stamp (WS-082 — to source)\n- **Export/trade pottery:** Blue-and-white jar (Jingdezhen Yuan, WS-020), jar with cover (Jun), footed bowl (Kashan), Ming dish (placeholder), basin by \"Master Potter A\" (Puebla, WS-022)\n- **Decorative pottery for elite/architectural:** Delft plate (WS-083), English delftware plate (WS-084), tile spandrel (Iznik, WS-042 — case, dropped)\n- **Archaeological sherds:** Lapita dentate-stamped (WS-032, to-license), Jōmon flame vessel (WS-005, case)\n",
"- **Density per chapter:** F1.5 (0), F1.6 (0), F1.7 (0), F1.8 (3), F1.9 (3), F1.9a (4), F1.10 (4), F1.11 (3), F1.12 (1), F1.13 (1)\n- **Bias:** Heavy concentration in F1.9a–F1.11; no ceramics in F1.5, F1.6, F1.7; underrepresented in Pacific/metallurgy chapters\n\n---\n\n### Textiles & Fibre Arts (13 objects)\n- **Embroidered/patterned cloth (elite/ceremonial):** Paracas mantle (WS-050), Paracas tunic (WS-051), Paracas turban band (WS-052), Wari tunic (WS-007), block-printed cotton (Mamluk Egypt, WS-006), child's coat with ducks (WS-101, case), roundels with hunters samite (WS-102)\n- **Woven/technical cloth:** Diné wearing blanket c. 1875 (WS-054), chief blanket rug (WS-055), jamdani sari (WS-072, to-license), patola double-ikat (WS-073, to-source), barkcloth Lau Islands (WS-033, rights-blocked)\n- **Cover/binding cloth (ritua/domestic):** Egypt cover with four trees (WS-043)\n- **Density per chapter:** F1.5 (0), F1.6 (0), F1.7 (5), F1.9 (2), F1.9a (1), F1.10 (2), F1.12a (2), F1.13 (1)\n",
"- **Bias:** Heavily concentrated in F1.7 (5 of 13); primarily Andean and Asian textiles; no Africa/Americas/Europe textiles except Diné and Egyptian; living traditions (Jamdani, Diné) underrepresented; barkcloth blocked\n\n---\n\n### Metals (Copper, Bronze, Iron, Gold, Silver, Brass) (20 objects)\n- **Casting (lost-wax, mould):** Nataraja Chola (WS-070), Shiva and Parvati Chola (WS-071), ancestral head Benin (WS-011), plaque Igun Eronmwon (WS-010)\n- **Forged/worked bronze:** Tripod cauldron Shang (WS-060), wine vessel Shang (WS-002), Master of Animals cheekpiece Luristan (WS-092), horse bit Luristan (WS-091, case)\n- **Brass/weight/decorative:** Gold weight (geometric, WS-012), gold weight (antelope, WS-013), ceremonial axe (WS-014), Iznik lamp (WS-040), Philippe-Joseph Brocard lamp (WS-045)\n- **Glass (mould-blown as metal substitute):** Amphoriskos (WS-093), janiform flask (WS-094), ennion cup (WS-095)\n- **Gold/precious:** Chavín plaque (WS-004)\n- **Density per chapter:** F1.5 (2), F1.6 (5), F1.8 (2), F1.9 (2), F1.9a (5), F1.10 (4), F1.11 (0)\n",
"- **Bias:** Heavily weighted toward West Asia (F1.9a: 5 objects) and Africa (F1.6: 5); Asia/steppe underrepresented (only WS-060 Shang cauldron); named makers rare (Brocard only); Africa shows highest variety in casting/work types (lost-wax, brass-casting, iron reuse)\n\n---\n\n### Stone & Worked Materials (5 objects)\n- **Carved/shaped tools & ritual:** Mason's chisel (Met, WS-003 — copper-alloy), ground-edge axe (Carpenter's Gap, WS-030, to-license)\n- **Carved tablet/container:** Cuneiform tablet (distribution of barley, WS-090)\n- **Seals/stamp:** Indus seal (WS-074, to-source)\n- **Density per chapter:** F1.5 (1 — Indus seal), F1.6 (0), F1.8 (0), F1.9a (2), F1.11 (0), F1.12 (1)\n- **Bias:** Extremely underrepresented (5 of 64); lowest-density material class; primarily Mesopotamian/Indus Valley\n\n---\n\n### Mixed Media / Composite Objects (4 objects)\n- **Ceramic with inlay:** Jun ware jar (WS-061), Goryeo vessels with inlay (WS-062, WS-063)\n- **Leather/metal composite:** Horse bit with bronze cheekpieces (WS-091, case)\n- **Density:** Across F1.8, F1.9a\n- **Bias:** Limited; most are East Asian ceramics\n\n---\n\n### Decorative/Luxury Objects (Non-Archaeological Living Traditions) (8 objects)\n",
"- **Signed modern work:** Wedgwood Portland Vase (WS-085), Brocard lamp (WS-045), delftware plates (WS-083, WS-084)\n- **Living traditions:** Diné weavers (WS-054, WS-055), Jamdani weavers (WS-072), Pazyryk felt context\n- **Enamelled glass:** Bottle Europe c. 1900 (WS-044)\n- **Density:** F1.7, F1.10, F1.11\n- **Bias:** Primarily European and living craft traditions; named makers limited to Brocard and Diné \"woman artist\" (attribution in progress)\n\n---\n\n### Paper/Printed Materials (3 objects)\n- **Printed objects:** One Million Pagodas (WS-008), Catalan Atlas (AS-003)\n- **Manuscripts/scrolls:** Oracle bone to-source (WS-X02, excluded as poorly dated)\n- **Density:** F1.5 (1), F1.8 (1), F1.13 (1)\n- **Bias:** Underrepresented; only one is East Asian (Pagodas); missing Islamic/Indian/African manuscripts in open-access; Catalan Atlas licence to check\n\n---\n\n### Objects with Inscription/Writing (9 objects)\n- **Text-bearing ceramics:** Wine vessel Father Yi (WS-002), footed bowl (WS-021), Meroitic cup (WS-016), terra sigillata bowl (WS-082)\n- **Carved inscription:** Indus seal (WS-074)\n",
"- **Printed/stamped text:** One Million Pagodas (WS-008), oracle bone (excluded), maker's mark (terra sigillata WS-082)\n- **Text + image:** Catalan Atlas sheet 6 (AS-003), footed bowl with calligraphy (WS-021)\n- **Density:** Concentrated in F1.5, F1.8, F1.9a, F1.11\n- **Significance:** Objects showing knowledge/proof of making/ownership; underrepresented in oral/craft traditions\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Object Classification by Function/Use\n\n### (1) Writing & Record-Keeping (9 objects)\n- **Cuneiform tablets:** WS-001, WS-090\n- **Oracle bones:** Excluded (WS-X02)\n- **Inscribed objects:** Wine vessel (WS-002), Indus seal (WS-074), terra sigillata (WS-082), Meroitic cup (WS-016)\n- **Printed texts:** One Million Pagodas (WS-008), Catalan Atlas (AS-003)\n- **Chapters:** F1.5 (2), F1.8 (1), F1.9a (1), F1.11 (1), F1.13 (1)\n- **Gap:** No African/Native American/Pacific writing systems exemplified\n\n---\n\n### (2) Container/Vessel for Domestic Use (12 objects)\n- **Storage/serving:** Jun jar (WS-061), Kashan footed bowl (WS-021), amphoriskos (WS-093), janiform flask (WS-094), Delft plates (WS-083, WS-084), English delftware (WS-084), Meroitic cup (WS-016), terra sigillata (WS-082)\n",
"- **Drinking/ritual:** Ennion cup signed (WS-095), black-figure amphora (WS-080, WS-081), footed bowl (WS-021)\n- **Chapters:** F1.9a (3), F1.10 (2), F1.11 (4), F1.8 (1), F1.9 (1)\n- **Variety:** Mediterranean, East Asian, Islamic, European; high density in F1.11 (Greece/Europe)\n\n---\n\n### (3) Textiles for Personal Wear & Adornment (10 objects)\n- **Clothing:** Paracas mantle (WS-050), tunic (WS-051), Wari tunic (WS-007), Diné blankets (WS-054, WS-055), child's coat (WS-101, case), cotton fragment (WS-006)\n- **Headwear:** Paracas turban band (WS-052)\n- **Sari:** Jamdani (WS-072)\n- **Chapters:** F1.5 (0), F1.7 (5), F1.9 (1), F1.9 (1), F1.10 (1), F1.12a (2)\n- **Gap:** Only Andean and Asia/America textiles; no African, European everyday textiles\n\n---\n\n### (4) Ritual/Ceremonial Objects (11 objects)\n- **Ancestral/commemorative:** Ancestral head Benin (WS-011)\n- **Religious:** Iznik lamp for mosque (WS-040), Nataraja Chola (WS-070), Shiva-Parvati (WS-071), cover with trees (WS-043), footed bowl Kashan (WS-021)\n- **Symbolic/exchange:** Plaque Igun Eronmwon (WS-010), One Million Pagodas (WS-008)\n- **Prestige/feast:** Throwing knife and sceptre (WS-015, colonial-gap)\n",
"- **Chapters:** F1.9a (2), F1.10 (3), F1.6 (1), F1.8 (1), F1.9 (1)\n- **Gap:** No Pacific, Oceanic, or Northwest Coast sacred regalia; low representation of African, Asian ritual\n\n---\n\n### (5) Craft Tools & Production (2 objects)\n- **Mason's chisel:** WS-003\n- **Axe/tool:** Carpenter's Gap ground-edge (WS-030)\n- **Chapters:** F1.5 (1), F1.6 (1), F1.12 (1 — to-license)\n- **Severe gap:** Production tools nearly absent from Atlas; no looms, dye-pots, kiln equipment, casting moulds, weaving samples\n\n---\n\n### (6) Harness, Equipment & Equestrian Objects (3 objects)\n- **Horse gear:** Horse bit Luristan (WS-091, case), Master of Animals cheekpiece (WS-092), gold weight as symbolic horse gear (WS-012 as prestige object)\n- **Chapters:** F1.9a (2 — harness), F1.6 (1 — weight)\n- **Gap:** No chariot wood/wheels; Sintashta chariot (WS-103) is to-license\n\n---\n\n### (7) Precious/High-Status Objects (Metals & Gold) (8 objects)\n- **Gold items:** Chavín plaque (WS-004), gold weights (WS-012, WS-013)\n- **Brass:** Plaques (WS-010), weights (WS-012, WS-013), ceremonial axe (WS-014), ancestral head (WS-011)\n- **Status markers:** Iznik lamps (WS-040, WS-045 — signed), Brocard bottle (WS-044)\n",
"- **Chapters:** F1.5 (1), F1.6 (4), F1.10 (3)\n- **Pattern:** Africa shows high density of precious-metal status markers; East Asia low\n\n---\n\n### (8) Decorative/Artistic Objects (Luxury, signed, for display) (8 objects)\n- **Signed/attributed:** Brocard lamp (WS-045), Wedgwood Portland Vase (WS-085), Diné \"woman artist\" (WS-054), terra sigillata \"Master Potter A\" (WS-082)\n- **Ornamental:** Black-figure vessels (WS-080, WS-081), delftware (WS-083, WS-084), Delft plate (WS-083)\n- **Chapters:** F1.10 (2), F1.11 (4), F1.8 (1), F1.7 (1 — Diné)\n- **Bias:** High proportion of signed European and living artisans; low representation of unnamed/anonymous makers (which is the majority of historical production)\n\n---\n\n### (9) Trade/Export Goods (6 objects)\n- **Ceramics for export:** Blue-and-white jar (Jingdezhen, WS-020), basin Puebla (WS-022)\n- **Textiles for trade:** Block-printed cotton (WS-006 Mamluk), roundels samite (WS-102), jamdani (WS-072), patola (WS-073)\n- **Chapters:** F1.9 (1), F1.13 (2), F1.13 (1), F1.9 (1), F1.12a (1)\n- **Pattern:** Concentrated in long-distance trade chapters (F1.13, F1.9); low in local production/consumption chapters\n\n---\n\n",
"### (10) Living Practices / Living Traditions (1 object)\n- **Jamdani sari by living weavers:** WS-072\n- **Chapters:** F1.9\n- **Severe gap:** No living traditions exemplified in most chapters; no living Diné weavers shown despite objects; no living Iznik potters; no living metalworkers shown\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Object Density by Chapter & Coverage Matrix\n\n### Chapter Distribution Table\n\n| Chapter | Title | Exemplars | Cases | To-Source | To-License | Rights-Blocked | Excluded | **Total** | **Material Coverage** |\n|---------|-------|-----------|-------|-----------|------------|---|----------|----------|-------------|\n| **F1.5** | Writing | 3 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 2 | **7** | Cuneiform (2), bronze vessel (1), Paracas textiles (1), Indus seal (1), oracle bone (excl.), Maya vase (excl.) |\n| **F1.6** | Metals | 5 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | **7** | Benin brass (2), gold weights (2), iron ceremonial axe (1), Meroitic cup (1), colonial-gap knife (1), Nok head (excl.) |\n| **F1.7** | Fibre Arts | 5 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **5** | Paracas embroidery (3), Wari tunic (1), Diné blankets (2) |\n",
"| **F1.8** | Technologies (?) | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **3** | Shang tripod, Jun jar, Goryeo inlay, pagoda (1 — to check if belongs), Jōmon flame pot (case) |\n| **F1.9** | Metalworking | 3 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **4** | Chola bronze (2), Indus seal (1 — to-source), Jamdani sari (1) |\n| **F1.9a** | Trade & Exchange | 5 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | **8** | Kashan pottery (1), glass (3), Luristan bronze (1, case), amphoriskos (1), Meroitic cup (1), Ennion cup (1), cuneiform tablet (1), Luristan cheekpiece (1) |\n| **F1.10** | Islamic World / Endowments | 4 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **5** | Iznik lamp & dish (2), cover Egypt (1), tile (case), Brocard lamp (1) |\n| **F1.11** | Mediterranean & Europe | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | **3** | Black-figure amphora (2), Delft/English delftware (2), Wedgwood vase (1) |\n| **F1.12** | Deep Time (Indigenous) | 1 | 0 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 0 | **5** | Lapita pottery (1), ground-edge axe (1 — to-license), stick chart (1 — rights-blocked), excavation placeholder |\n| **F1.12a** | Horses & Mobility | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | **4** | Samite roundels (1), child's coat (case), Pazyryk carpet (1 — to-license), Sintashta chariot (1 — to-license) |\n",
"| **F1.13** | Long-Distance Trade | 2 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 | **4** | Jingdezhen jar (1), Puebla basin (1), Catalan Atlas (1 — to check), Belitung cargo (excl.) |\n| **F1.13 Networks** | Networks | (integrated) | | | | | | **(0 separate)** | |\n| **TOTAL** | | **37 exemplars** | **3 cases** | **5 to-source** | **4 to-license** | **1 rights-blocked** | **4 excluded** | **54** | |\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Critical Gaps & Density Imbalances\n\n### By Material Type:\n| Material | Count | Gap | Priority |\n|----------|-------|-----|----------|\n| **Ceramics** | 13 | Concentrated F1.9a–F1.11; none in F1.5–F1.7 or F1.12–F1.13 | HIGH — missing Jōmon pottery (case), no Pacific or African ceramics |\n| **Textiles** | 13 | Concentrated F1.7 (Andean); no Africa/Europe everyday cloth; living practices absent | HIGH — Mingei blocked, barkcloth blocked, Jamdani only partial |\n| **Metals** | 20 | Heavy F1.6 (Africa brass/gold), F1.9a (West Asia); Asia/steppe low | MEDIUM — Pazyryk blocked, Ordos to-source, East Asia light |\n| **Stone/Tools** | 5 | Extremely underrepresented; mostly tools not craft | HIGH — axes need expansion, no grinding stones/querns, no production tools |\n",
"| **Paper/Print** | 3 | No Islamic/Indian manuscripts; Catalan licence uncertain | HIGH — Arabic, Sanskrit, Chinese texts absent |\n| **Inscribed objects** | 9 | Cuneiform & Mediterranean heavy; no indigenous scripts | HIGH — no Egyptian, Mayan, Indus interpretation; oracle bone excluded |\n| **Living traditions** | 1 | Only Jamdani; Diné, Iznik, Pazyryk craftspeople absent | CRITICAL — most modern cultures have no exemplar |\n| **Production tools** | 2 | Tools underrepresented; no looms, moulds, kilns | CRITICAL — cannot show \"how it was made\" for most chapters |\n\n---\n\n### By Function/Use:\n| Function | Count | Gap | Priority |\n|----------|-------|-----|----------|\n| **Writing/record** | 9 | Mediterranean, Mesopotamian heavy; no indigenous writing systems shown | HIGH |\n| **Domestic containers** | 12 | Mediterranean/Islamic/East Asian; no Pacific, sub-Saharan, Americas everyday vessels | HIGH |\n| **Personal wear** | 10 | Andean-heavy (5); no African dress, European dress, Asian living tradition dress | HIGH |\n| **Ritual/ceremonial** | 11 | No Pacific sacred items (U'mista blocked); Benin plaque is contested repatriation case | MEDIUM |\n",
"| **Production tools** | 2 | Nearly absent; this is the lowest-count function class | CRITICAL |\n| **Harness/equestrian** | 3 | Luristan/gold weights only; no living horse cultures | MEDIUM |\n| **Precious objects** | 8 | Heavy Africa/Islamic; sparse East Asia | LOW |\n| **Decorative/artistic** | 8 | High European signed/named makers (bias); low anonymous/collective | MEDIUM |\n| **Trade goods** | 6 | Concentrated in trade chapters; low in source regions | MEDIUM |\n| **Living practices** | 1 | Only Jamdani; critical gap across most regions | CRITICAL |\n\n---\n\n### By Social Register:\n| Register | Count | Distribution | Gap |\n|----------|-------|---------------|-----|\n| **Household/everyday** | 12 | Concentrated Mediterranean, East Asia; underrepresented Africa, Americas, Pacific | HIGH |\n| **Elite/court** | 10 | Fairly even across F1.5–F1.10; F1.12–F1.13 sparse | MEDIUM |\n| **Ritual/sacred** | 11 | Concentration F1.10 (Islamic); missing indigenous/Pacific/African sacred context | MEDIUM |\n| **Guild/craft** | 4 | Terracotta (WS-002 guild vessel, WS-045 Brocard), Diné, Jamdani | HIGH — mostly living/modern |\n",
"| **Military/status** | 5 | Horse gear (3), gold weights (2); extremely narrow | LOW |\n| **Merchant/trader** | 6 | Connected to trade chapters; under-contextualized individual traders | MEDIUM |\n| **Anonymous/collective** | 25 | Majority; includes \"woman artist\" Diné (attribution uncertain), unattributed Paracas, guild members | MEDIUM — many items should list maker guilds, not just anonymity |\n\n---\n\n### By Maker Type:\n| Maker Type | Count | Examples | Bias |\n|------------|-------|----------|------|\n| **Named individual maker** | 4 | Ennion (glass cup), Brocard (lamp), Diné \"woman artist\", terra sigillata \"Master Potter A\" | LOW — only named makers shown are Greco-Roman, European modern, or living |\n| **Guild/collective** | 5 | Igun Eronmwon (Benin plaque), Shang bronze casters, Jingdezhen kiln, Kashan potters, Puebla \"Master Potter A\" | MEDIUM — guild identity inconsistently recorded |\n| **Artisan families** | 1 | Jamdani weavers (living) | LOW — no historical artisan families shown except living Jamdani |\n| **Anonymous** | 38 | Majority of all objects | VERY HIGH — no breakdown by gender, status, apprenticeship, training system |\n",
"| **Living practitioner** | 1 | Jamdani | CRITICAL GAP — no living Diné, Iznik, Pazyryk tradition shown despite historical objects |\n| **Documented death date / lifetime** | 8 | Brocard, Ennion, Diné \"woman artist\", implied artisans (Shang, Kashan, Jingdezhen) | LOW — most artisans' lifespans unknown |\n\n---\n\n### By Period:\n| Period | Count | Density | Gap |\n|--------|-------|---------|-----|\n| **Prehistory (45,000–3100 BCE)** | 2 | Ground-edge axe, oracle bone (excl.) | LOW — no Venus figurines, hand stencils, early textiles |\n| **Ancient (3100 BCE–476 CE)** | 18 | Heavy Mesopotamia (2), Egypt (3), Mediterranean (4), East Asia (4), Americas (2) | MEDIUM — balanced but no sub-Saharan, Pacific, steppe |\n| **Medieval (476–1453 CE)** | 15 | Islamic (3), East Asia (5), Europe (2), Americas (1), steppe (1) | MEDIUM — Islamic slightly overrepresented |\n| **Early Modern (1453–1800)** | 10 | Americas (2), East Asia (2), Europe (4), Islamic (1), India (1) | MEDIUM — European high for period size |\n| **Modern (1800+)** | 8 | Diné (2), European (2), living practices (1), signed modern (2), Egypt (1) | LOW — living traditions nearly absent |\n",
"| **Living (contemporary)** | 1 | Jamdani | CRITICAL GAP |\n\n---\n\n### By Geography:\n| Region | Count | Density | Chapters | Gap |\n|--------|-------|---------|----------|-----|\n| **Mesopotamia** | 3 | F1.5, F1.9a | 2 chapters | LOW — one object per chapter; no palace, temple, city context |\n| **Egypt** | 3 | F1.5, F1.6, F1.10 | 3 chapters | LOW — scattered; no daily life, no pyramid context, no Nubian collaboration shown |\n| **East Asia (China, Japan, Korea)** | 10 | F1.5, F1.8 (5), F1.9a | 3 chapters | MEDIUM — concentrated in technologies chapter; no living tradition |\n| **India & South Asia** | 5 | F1.9 (3), F1.9a (1) | 2 chapters | LOW — Chola bronze (2), Indus seal (1 — to-source), Jamdani (1) |\n| **Islamic World & Iran** | 7 | F1.9a (4), F1.10 (3) | 2 chapters | MEDIUM — concentrated in trade and endowment chapters |\n| **Mediterranean & Europe** | 10 | F1.9a (3), F1.11 (3), F1.10 (2), F1.8 (1) | 4 chapters | MEDIUM — scattered; Mediterranean high, no medieval trade centers (Venice, Genoa) shown |\n| **Africa** | 5 | F1.6 (5) | 1 chapter | LOW — only Benin and Nubia shown; no West African textiles, no East African trade, no Ethiopian texts |\n",
"| **Americas (Andean)** | 7 | F1.5, F1.7 (5), F1.9a | 2 chapters | MEDIUM — Paracas/Wari/Inka heavy; no Mesoamerica exemplars |\n| **Americas (Other)** | 2 | F1.7 (Diné), F1.13 (Puebla) | 2 chapters | LOW — Diné (2), Mesoamerica (1 — Puebla); no North American indigenous, no South American beyond Andes |\n| **Pacific & Oceania** | 2 | F1.12 (2, both blocked/to-license) | 1 chapter | CRITICAL — stick chart blocked, Lapita to-license, barkcloth blocked, ground-edge axe to-license |\n| **Steppe & Central Asia** | 4 | F1.12a (2), F1.9a (1), F1.8 (1) | 2 chapters | LOW — Luristan (2), samite roundels (1), Jun jar Asian origin (1) |\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: Variety Matrix Recommendations\n\n### Proposed Minimum Variety Per Chapter (to reduce density bias):\n\n| Chapter | Current | Recommended Expansion | Priority Items |\n|---------|---------|----------------------|-----------------|\n| **F1.5** | 7 | +3: add 1 African object, 1 Pacific/Oceanic, 1 American indigenous; move Indus seal to F1.9 | African: gold weight or architectural context; Pacific: stick chart or Lapita |\n",
"| **F1.6** | 7 | +2: add 1 East Asian metalwork (Shang bronze), 1 American metalwork (Chavín to F1.5, add another) | Shang casting (WS-060 cauldron already in F1.8, move one); Americas: Chavín plaque already listed |\n| **F1.7** | 5 | +3: add 1 African textile (Mali indigo, Yoruba adire), 1 European textile (linen production), 1 Asia textile living tradition | Africa: to-source; Europe: Commons or museum; Asia: living Jamdani or historic silk |\n| **F1.8** | 3 | +2: add 1 African production technique (pottery kiln context), 1 American production context | Africa: ceramic firing context; Americas: weaving loom or dye-pot |\n| **F1.9** | 4 | +2: add 1 African metalwork, 1 American metalwork; clarify chapter scope | Africa: Ife or Sapi-Portuguese ivory carving; Americas: Chavín gold (move from F1.5) or Mesoamerican casting |\n| **F1.9a** | 8 | +2: add 1 East Asian trade object (Chinese celadon or porcelain), 1 African trade object | East Asia: Goryeo celadon or Ming dish exemplar; Africa: Ethiopian gold, Indian Ocean trade cloth |\n",
"| **F1.10** | 5 | +2: add 1 African Islamic object (Mali cloth, Timbuktu manuscript), 1 Asian Islamic object (Indian/ Persian) | Africa: manuscript or cloth exemplar; Asia: to-source |\n| **F1.11** | 3 | +3: add 1 Mediterranean trade object, 1 indigenous American object (Taíno, Northwest Coast — need consultation), 1 Asian export ware | Mediterranean: Roman trade pot or mosaic; Americas: sacred regalia note (not reproduction); Asia: blue-and-white for Europe |\n| **F1.12** | 5 | +4: add 1 African tool/site, 1 Asian site, 1 European cave/rock art, 1 South American site (beyond Andes) | Deep time: Madjedbebe (Mirarr consultation), African stone tools, Asian rock art sites |\n| **F1.12a** | 4 | +2: add 1 African mobility object (Saharan camel gear), 1 American mobility object (horse adoption, Comanche) | Africa: Tuareg or Bedouin equipment; Americas: Comanche/Apache, Spanish horse adoption |\n| **F1.13** | 4 | +3: add 1 African trade good, 1 Pacific Island trade good, 1 Mediterranean/Indian Ocean map or document | Africa: Swahili trade cloth or gold; Pacific: Polynesian exchange goods; Atlantic: slaveship manifest (sensitive) |\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: Object Density Insights\n\n",
"### Highest Density Chapters:\n1. **F1.9a (8 objects):** Trade & Exchange — dominated by ceramics, glass, metals; strongest in West Asia\n2. **F1.5 (7) & F1.6 (7):** Writing and Metals — tied; F1.5 weighted Mesopotamia, F1.6 weighted Africa\n3. **F1.7 (5), F1.10 (5), F1.12 (5), F1.12a (4):** Lower-density clusters; F1.7 is Andean-heavy\n\n### Lowest Density Chapters:\n1. **F1.11 (3):** Mediterranean & Europe — surprisingly light given historical significance\n2. **F1.8 (3):** Technologies — unclear scope (Jōmon, Shang, Jun, Goryeo, Pagoda — all East Asian, all medieval+)\n\n### Overall Observation:\n**Object density does not reflect chapter importance.** F1.11 (Mediterranean & Europe, 3 objects) is historically dense but materially light; F1.9a (West Asian trade, 8 objects) is materially heavy but geographically narrow. This suggests chapter assignment is not driven by object count but by conceptual focus.\n\n---\n\n## Part 7: Recommendations for Task 27 (Movements & Civilisations Deep-Dive Decision)\n\n### Key Finding:\n**The current object set does NOT support independent civilisation or movement deep-dives.**\n\n",
"- **Nok (F1.6):** 1 object to-check (WS-X04, excluded); needs separate iconography/ritual context chapter\n- **Chola (F1.9):** 2 bronze sculptures (WS-070, WS-071); insufficient for a standalone chapter on South Indian metalwork, trade, or religious practice\n- **Jingdezhen (F1.13):** 1 blue-and-white jar (WS-020); needs context on kiln evolution, imperial patronage, export markets\n- **Iznik (F1.10):** 2 objects (WS-040, WS-041 provisional); insufficient for Ottoman ceramics, waqf institutions, or imperial taste\n- **Paracas (F1.5, F1.7):** 3 objects (WS-050, WS-051, WS-052); tied to Andean fibre tradition; no Paracas-specific deep dive shown\n- **Benin (F1.6, F1.13):** 1 exemplar + 1 excluded (WS-010, WS-X04 contextual); insufficient for Benin court metalworking, colonial seizure, or repatriation\n\n### Implication:\n**Movements (Mingei, Indian Ocean trade, waqf, Silk Road, horse domestication) can be shown across chapters with cross-referencing and context cards.** **Civilisations (Nok, Chola, Jingdezhen, Iznik, Benin) currently do NOT have sufficient material density for independent deep-dive chapters.**\n\n",
"**Decision required (Task 27):** Should the Atlas add dedicated objects and chapters for major civilisations, or teach them through comparative cross-chapter analysis and contextual deep-dives on themes (e.g., \"Royal Workshops,\" \"Bureaucratic Control,\" \"Sacred Iconography\")?\n\n"
