"# Task 27: Movements & Civilisations Treatment — Should They Be Separate Deep-Dives?\n**Status:** Strategic decision framework  \n**Date:** 2026-09-28  \n**Depends on:** Task 26 findings (object density analysis)  \n**Outputs for:** Template v3 structure, Historian pack v2 rules\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Definitions & Scope\n\n### What is a \"Civilisation\" in F1 Atlas context?\nA **civilisation** = a geographically-rooted culture with:\n- Named polity or cultural identity (e.g., Nok, Chola, Benin, Iznik, Jingdezhen, Minoan, Maya, Indus Valley)\n- Recognizable material signature (ceramic style, iconography, technology, language)\n- Distinct time span (flourished for centuries+; WS-043 \"Egyptian civilization\" versus WS-016 \"Meroitic civilization\")\n- Documented succession or cultural inheritance (e.g., Chola metalwork → Tamil Nadu metalwork tradition)\n\n**Examples in current register:**\n- **Nok** (F1.6): Nigeria, 1500 BCE–turn of era; terracotta figurines, funerary context\n- **Indus Valley** (F1.5 & F1.9): c. 2500 BCE seals; undeciphered script\n- **Chola** (F1.9): South India, c. 900–1300 CE; lost-wax bronze casting, religious patronage\n",
"- **Maya** (F1.5 excluded case): Mesoamerica, 2000 BCE–1600s CE; hieroglyphic writing, calendrical astronomy\n- **Iznik** (F1.10): Ottoman Turkey, 1450–1700s; blue-and-white ceramics, architectural tiles\n- **Jingdezhen** (F1.13): China, 1300s+; blue-and-white porcelain, imperial monopoly\n- **Benin** (F1.6 & F1.13): West Africa, 1400s–1897; brass plaques, royal court, colonial seizure\n- **Paracas** (F1.5 & F1.7): South Peru, 800–100 BCE; embroidered textiles, bundle burials\n- **Wari** (F1.7): South Peru, 650–1000 CE; tapestry tunics, administrative expansion\n- **Mediterranean (Attic)** (F1.11): Greece, 6th–5th century BCE; black-figure pottery, workshop organization\n- **Diné** (F1.7): North America, Navajo; weaving, horse-adoption, contemporary tradition\n\n**NOT in register but could be:**\n- Minoan (Mediterranean, no objects)\n- Mycenaean (Mediterranean, no objects)\n- Cahokia Mississippian (Americas, only landscape)\n- Egyptian New Kingdom (Egypt has 3 objects scattered across chapters)\n- Islamic Caliphate (scattered across F1.10, F1.13)\n- Sung/Ming China (multiple objects in F1.8, F1.13)\n\n---\n\n### What is a \"Movement\" in F1 Atlas context?\n",
"A **movement** = a trans-geographical practice, aesthetic, or economic system that crosses cultural/political boundaries:\n- **Mingei (folk crafts):** Japan, 1920s+; philosophy of anonymous beauty; crosses ceramics, textiles, metalwork\n- **Indian Ocean trade network:** 1000–1800s; connects Africa, Arabia, India, Southeast Asia, China; shows comparative scarcity model\n- **Waqf (Islamic endowment):** religious legal system affecting architecture, ceramics, water systems; spans Mediterranean to Central Asia\n- **Silk Road / Long-Distance Trade:** 1000 BCE–1800s CE; textiles, metals, ceramics across Eurasia\n- **Horse domestication & pastoralism:** Central Asia, 3500 BCE+; spreads via steppe peoples to Mediterranean, India, Europe\n- **Arts and Crafts movement:** Europe/America, 1880s–1920s; reactions to industrialization; connects Brocard, William Morris, Mingei aesthetics\n- **Camelid domestication & Andean textile tradition:** South America, pre-Columbian; connects Paracas, Wari, Inka via fibre arts\n- **Mamluk aesthetics:** Mediterranean/Islamic, 1200–1500s; ceramics, glass, architecture; military-administrative network\n",
"- **Colonialism & collection:** 1500s–1900s; affects provenance, repatriation, museum holdings (WS-010 Benin plaque, WS-X04 Nok head, WS-X03 Maya vase)\n\n**Current treatment in Atlas:**\n- **Mingei:** Appears as support material concept; no dedicated chapter (blocked by copyright)\n- **Indian Ocean trade:** F1.13 Networks shows connections; objects sparse\n- **Waqf:** F1.10 mentions endowed building (Iznik lamp); not explicitly taught\n- **Silk Road:** Implied in F1.12a, F1.13; no dedicated treatment\n- **Horse domestication:** F1.12a chapter dedicated to it; weak in objects\n- **Arts and Crafts:** Not explicitly taught; appears in Brocard lamp and Mingei philosophy\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Current State Analysis — Object Density by Civilisation\n\n### Civilisations with Dedicated Chapter(s):\n| Civilisation | Chapter(s) | Objects | Status | Issue |\n|--------------|-----------|---------|--------|-------|\n| **Nok** | F1.6 | 1 (WS-X04, excluded) | Case only | No exemplar; funerary context sensitive |\n| **Chola** | F1.9 | 2 (WS-070, WS-071) | Exemplar | Insufficient for independent study; no architecture, no trade context, no administration shown |\n",
"| **Iznik** | F1.10 | 2 (WS-040, WS-041) | Exemplar + Provisional | Weak exemplification; tile (case) dropped; dish (enquiry status) provisional |\n| **Jingdezhen** | F1.13 | 1 (WS-020) | Exemplar | Blue-and-white jar only; no kiln context, no imperial commission, no export market shown |\n| **Benin** | F1.6 & F1.13 | 2 (WS-010 exemplar, WS-X04 excluded case) | Mixed | Exemplar is 1500–1600s plaque; excluded head is latter-day and contested; no royal court context shown |\n| **Paracas** | F1.5 & F1.7 | 3 (WS-050, WS-051, WS-052) | Exemplar (tied to F1.7 topic) | All are textiles; no ceramics, no administration, no burial-mound archaeology shown |\n| **Wari** | F1.7 | 1 (WS-007) | Exemplar | Single tunic; no administrative architecture, no state organization shown |\n| **Mediterranean/Attic** | F1.11 | 2 (WS-080, WS-081) | Exemplar | Black-figure ware only; no Archaic or Classical sculpture, no pottery production context, no democracy/citizenship shown |\n| **Diné** | F1.7 | 2 (WS-054, WS-055) | Exemplar | Living tradition; textiles exemplified; no language, no origin narratives, no post-1868 history shown |\n\n### Civilisations WITHOUT Dedicated Chapters:\n",
"| Civilisation | Objects | Current Chapter(s) | Issue |\n|--------------|---------|-------------------|-------|\n| **Indus Valley** | 1 (WS-074, to-source) | F1.5 & F1.9 | Seals only; undeciphered writing; no architecture, no urbanism, no trade shown |\n| **Maya** | 1 (WS-X03, excluded case) | F1.5 only (as case study in F1.24 looting) | Exemplar excluded for provenance; only taught as problematic case, not as civilization |\n| **Egyptian Kingdoms** | 3 scattered | F1.5 (cuneiform neighbor), F1.6 (Meroitic cup), F1.10 (cloth cover) | No dedicated chapter despite 3,000-year history; objects unrelated by period/category |\n| **Minoan** | 0 | — | Missing entirely; important Mediterranean precedent |\n| **Cahokia Mississippian** | 1 (landscape only) | F1.12 | No artifacts; only landscape/mounds; no administration, no platform mound architecture shown |\n| **Islamic Caliphate** | Scattered (Iznik, Kashan, glass, cover cloth) | F1.10 (Iznik, cover, lamp), F1.9a (Kashan, glass) | Objects scattered; no political/legal/administrative structure shown (waqf, caliphate, sultanate not exemplified) |\n",
"| **Sung/Ming Dynasty** | Multiple (Jun, celadon, blue-and-white) | F1.8 (Jun), F1.13 (Jingdezhen) | Scattered across chapters; no imperial workshop context, no Forbidden City, no exam system shown |\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: How Other Programs Treat Civilisations & Movements\n\n### Benchmark A: Museum Wing Model (e.g., Metropolitan Museum of Art)\n**Structure:** Dedicated galleries by civilisation/period\n- **Egyptian Wing:** 2,000+ objects organized by dynasty\n- **Islamic Art Wing:** Carpets, ceramics, glass, metalwork organized by region/period, not by movement\n- **American Wing:** Organized by region/time, then by type\n- **Asian Art Wing:** China, Japan, Korea organized by period, not by movement\n\n**Approach:** Each civilisation gets a dedicated space with objects organized chronologically and by type. Movements (trade, migration, influence) shown through labels but not through dedicated sub-galleries.\n\n**Implication for F1:** Civilisations would need 3–5+ objects each to justify a chapter.\n\n---\n\n### Benchmark B: Academic Textbook Model (e.g., \"The History of Art\" survey)\n**Structure:** Chapters organized by theme OR period OR region\n",
"- **World Archaeology survey books:** Often use chapters for broad regions (Mediterranean, Africa, Americas) with sub-sections for major civilisations (Minoan, Classical Greece, Hellenistic)\n- **History of Technology:** Organized by technology (metallurgy, weaving, printing) with sub-sections on civilisations that innovated (Hittites for iron, Jingdezhen for porcelain)\n- **History of Trade:** Organized by trade network (Silk Road, Indian Ocean) with embedded civilisations (Chola, Iznik, Jingdezhen shown as nodes in network)\n\n**Implication for F1:** If organized by theme (e.g., \"Casting,\" \"Weaving,\" \"Writing\"), civilisations appear as examples. If organized by movement, civilisations appear as nodes.\n\n---\n\n### Benchmark C: Encyclopaedic Atlas Model (e.g., British Museum object-by-object model)\n**Structure:** Each object gets a detailed card with context including civilisation, period, material, technique, trade connections\n- British Museum online: Each Benin plaque card mentions \"Benin Kingdom, 1400–1897\" but does NOT have a dedicated \"Benin Kingdom\" chapter; kingdom information is on each card\n",
"- Smithsonian online: Similar; each item contextualized but not compiled into a civilisation chapter\n\n**Implication for F1:** Civilisations taught via cross-referenced object cards; no dedicated deep-dive unless objects appear across multiple chapters and require synthesis.\n\n---\n\n### Benchmark D: Educational Model (e.g., Khan Academy Art History)\n**Structure:** Mix of short videos on civilisations + thematic lessons on techniques/trade\n- **Civilisation videos:** 5–15 min overviews of Nok, Egypt, Maya, Islamic World with 2–5 representative objects each\n- **Thematic lessons:** \"Trade in the Indian Ocean,\" \"Metalworking Techniques,\" \"Women Artists\"\n- Objects appear in both contexts\n\n**Implication for F1:** Civilisations could get short deep-dives (500–1000 words) with 2–3 objects each, plus separate thematic deep-dives (1000–2000 words) on movements.\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Current F1 Atlas Structure & Constraints\n\n### Current Model:\n**Chapters F1.5–F1.13 + F1.13 Networks are organized by THEME, not by civilisation or geography.**\n- F1.5: Writing (cuneiform, Shang bronze, Paracas, Indus seals, oracle bone)\n",
"- F1.6: Metals (Benin brass, Egypt copper tool, gold weights, Meroitic cup, Nok head — excluded)\n- F1.7: Fibre Arts (Paracas textiles, Wari tunic, Diné blankets)\n- F1.8: Technologies (Shang, Jun, Goryeo, Jōmon — unclear theme)\n- F1.9: Metalworking (Chola bronze)\n- F1.9a: Trade & Exchange (ceramics, glass, metals from West Asia, Mediterranean, Egypt)\n- F1.10: Islamic World / Endowments (Iznik, Kashan, Egypt cover, Brocard, glass)\n- F1.11: Mediterranean & Europe (Attic black-figure, Delft, Wedgwood)\n- F1.12: Deep Time / Indigenous (Carpenter's Gap, Lapita, Madjedbebe, wayfinding practice)\n- F1.12a: Horses & Mobility (Luristan bits, samite roundels, Pazyryk, Sintashta)\n- F1.13: Long-Distance Trade (Jingdezhen, Puebla, Catalan Atlas, networks)\n\n**Problem:** Chapter themes are neither purely thematic (not \"Metalworking\" throughout) nor purely geographic/civilisational (not \"Islamic World\" exclusively in F1.10).\n\n",
"**Implication:** The current structure is **hybrid and transitional**—it vacillates between theme and geography, making it unclear whether a new civilisation chapter should be geographic (e.g., \"Benin Kingdom\"), thematic (e.g., \"Royal Workshops\"), or movement-based (e.g., \"Slave Trade Routes\").\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: Analysis — Can Civilisations Be Taught Without Dedicated Deep-Dive Chapters?\n\n### YES, if:\n1. **Objects are cross-referenced across chapters** (e.g., Chola bronzes appear in F1.9 \"Metalworking\" with a link to \"F1.X Chola South Indian Metalworking\" brief)\n2. **Context cards detail civilisation info** (e.g., each Nok object card says \"Nok Culture, central Nigeria, 1500 BCE–turn of era; funerary context; Frankfurt excavation shows burial ritual; Lagos Museum holds comparative collection\")\n3. **\"Grouping pages\" aggregate related objects by civilisation** (e.g., \"Nok in the Atlas: 1 exemplar case + 2 contextual papers + 1 exclusion case; what each teaches\")\n4. **Movements are emphasized over civilisations** (e.g., \"Chola Kingdom as node in Indian Ocean metalworking network\" rather than \"Chola Kingdom as independent civilization\")\n",
"5. **Comparison is built in** (e.g., \"Iznik and Jingdezhen: Two Imperial Ceramic Workshops Compared\" as a movement-level deep-dive, not two separate civilisation chapters)\n\n### NO, if:\n1. **Learners expect a dedicated civilisation chapter** for every major culture (Nok, Chola, Benin, Maya, Egypt, China, etc.)\n2. **Civilisation-specific knowledge is taught** (administration, politics, succession, gender roles, labor, daily life — none currently exemplified in objects)\n3. **Objects must be grouped by civilisation, not by theme** (would require reorganizing F1.5–F1.13)\n4. **Independent deep dives are required** to show \"breadth of objects, concepts, influences, effects, scale, impact\" per user request\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: Recommended Approach\n\n### Decision: **Hybrid Model with Thematic Chapters + Civilisation Deep-Dive Briefs**\n\n**Structure:**\n1. **Keep F1.5–F1.13 as thematic chapters** (Writing, Metals, Fibre Arts, Technologies, Metalworking, Trade & Exchange, Islamic World, Mediterranean, Deep Time, Horses, Long-Distance Trade)\n2. **Add 3–5 civilisation-focused deep-dive briefs** (750–1500 words each) for civilisations with 2+ objects in the Atlas:\n",
"   - **Nok** (F1.6): \"Funerary Sculpture as Political Authority—Contested Origin, Settled Dating\"\n   - **Chola** (F1.9): \"Sacred Bronzes, Royal Patronage, and Metalworking Guilds\"\n   - **Iznik** (F1.10): \"Ottoman Imperial Ceramics and the Waqf System\"\n   - **Jingdezhen** (F1.13): \"Imperial Monopoly, Export Markets, and Blue-and-White Porcelain\"\n   - **Benin** (F1.6 & F1.13): \"Royal Plaques, Colonial Seizure, and Repatriation Cases\"\n3. **Add 2–3 movement-focused deep-dive briefs** (1000–2000 words each):\n   - **Indian Ocean Trade Network** (F1.13 Networks): \"Chola Bronzes, Kashan Ceramics, and Swahili Trade Routes\"\n   - **Waqf and Endowed Buildings** (F1.10 & beyond): \"Iznik Tiles, Religious Architecture, and Civic Authority\"\n   - **Horses, Mobility, and Cultural Exchange** (F1.12a & beyond): \"Steppe Pastoralism, Chariot Warfare, and Animal Art Across Eurasia\"\n4. **Link all objects to both their thematic chapter AND their civilisation/movement brief** (via cross-reference cards)\n5. **Grouping pages aggregate civilisation-level cases** (as already designed for Nok, Mingei, Igun Eronmwon)\n\n",
"**Advantage:** Maintains thematic clarity while adding civilisation context; avoids creating under-resourced civilisation chapters (current Nok has only 1 case, Chola has only 2 bronzes).\n\n---\n\n## Part 7: Implementation Rules for Historian Pack v2\n\n### Rule P13: Civilisation Identity\n**When to flag an object as belonging to a civilisation:**\n- Object dates within civilisation's known flourishing period (e.g., Nok, 1500 BCE–turn of era)\n- Object shows recognizable material/stylistic signature of that civilisation (e.g., Nok terracotta, Chola bronze, Iznik blue-and-white)\n- Civilisation has 2+ objects in the Atlas\n\n**Required fields:**\n- `civilisation_name`: \"Nok\" or \"Chola\" or null\n- `civilisation_period`: \"1500 BCE–turn of era\" (or years only if approximate)\n- `civilisation_location`: \"central Nigeria\" or \"Tamil Nadu, South India\"\n- `civilisation_context_brief`: \"Funerary figurines show ritual authority\" or \"Lost-wax casting for temple bronze ritual\"\n\n**Example (WS-X04 Nok head, excluded):**\n```json\n{\n  \"civilisation_name\": \"Nok\",\n  \"civilisation_period\": \"1500 BCE–turn of era (actual date 20–620 CE, post-Nok)\",\n  \"civilisation_location\": \"central Nigeria, Plateau State\",\n",
"  \"civilisation_context_brief\": \"Thermoluminescence dated 20–620 CE, after Nok culture ended per Frankfurt excavation chronology; first recorded Brussels dealer 1994\",\n  \"sensitivity_flag\": \"funerary\",\n  \"exclusion_reason\": \"post-civilisation date; belongs to 'Late Iron Age Nigeria' not Nok\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Rule P14: Movement Identity\n**When to flag an object as belonging to a movement:**\n- Object participates in a cross-civilisation practice (e.g., Indian Ocean trade, waqf patronage, horse adoption, long-distance exchange)\n- Object demonstrates technique or aesthetic that crosses political boundaries (e.g., blue-and-white porcelain adopted across Islamic world)\n- Multiple objects (2+) in Atlas show the same movement\n\n**Required fields:**\n- `movement_name`: \"Indian Ocean Trade\" or \"Waqf Endowment\" or \"Horse Domestication\" or null\n- `movement_scope`: \"regional\" (within 1000 miles) or \"continental\" (Eurasia) or \"global\" (multiple continents)\n- `movement_period`: \"1000–1800 CE\" (when movement was active)\n- `movement_role`: \"producer\" or \"trader\" or \"consumer\" or \"adapter\" or \"innovator\"\n\n**Example (WS-020 Jingdezhen jar, F1.13):**\n```json\n{\n",
"  \"movement_name\": \"Chinese Porcelain Export Trade\",\n  \"movement_scope\": \"global\",\n  \"movement_period\": \"1300s+\",\n  \"movement_role\": \"producer\",\n  \"movement_context\": \"Yuan blue-and-white for Islamic and European markets; source of European imitation (Delft)\"\n}\n```\n\n**Example (WS-040 Iznik lamp, F1.10):**\n```json\n{\n  \"movement_name\": \"Waqf Endowment System\",\n  \"movement_scope\": \"continental\",\n  \"movement_period\": \"1100s–1800s\",\n  \"movement_role\": \"commissioned_object\",\n  \"movement_context\": \"Mosque lamp made for endowed building; shows continuity of Islamic architectural patronage across Ottoman empire\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Rule P15: Funerary & Ancestral Material Rule\n**Decision on how to show burial/funerary context:**\n\n**IF civilisation's primary material signature is funerary (e.g., Nok terracotta figurines from burials, Paracas mummy-bundle textiles, Egyptian coffins):**\n- **INCLUDE the funerary context in the card** (e.g., \"Nok figurines were placed in burial chambers to mark grave sites and commemorate authority\")\n- **MARK as sensitivity flag:** \"burial_context\" or \"ancestral_material\"\n",
"- **ADD consultation note:** \"Nok objects not repatriated; held in African museums and public collections; no known current custodian requesting return\"\n- **TEACH the practice:** \"Funerary Art as Political Authority\" or \"Burial Ritual as Social Hierarchy\"\n\n**IF object is funerary but civilisation also has non-funerary material:**\n- **PREFER non-funerary exemplar** (e.g., use living Chola temple bronze instead of funerary figurine; use household Nok pot instead of burial Nok head)\n- **IF only funerary example available:** Mark as sensitivity flag and note gap in open-access corpus\n\n**IF object is funerary and civilisation is otherwise unknown:**\n- **TEACH with care:** Flag as \"burial/funerary context,\" note that knowledge comes from excavation/tomb, acknowledge indigenous perspectives on ancestor disturbance\n- **EXAMPLE:** \"Teouma Lapita dish, Vanuatu; funerary context; EXCLUDE—ancestral material, Lapita people may object to display; teach via academic paper on mortuary practices instead\"\n\n**Current status:**\n- **Nok head (WS-X04):** Excluded; funerary, post-Nok date\n- **Paracas textiles (WS-050, WS-051, WS-052):** Included; bundle burial context taught as intentional practice\n",
"- **Cahokia Mound 72:** Excluded; burial remains\n- **Madagascar lamba (WS-? — to-check):** Flagged as burial shroud; needs consultation\n- **Teouma Lapita (WS-032?):** To-license; flagged as funerary; recommend exclusion\n\n**Recommendation:** Include funerary context when it is THE primary way we know the civilisation (Nok, Paracas, Egyptian, Cahokia); teach it as intentional social practice, not as disturbing ancestors. BUT exclude human remains (Cahokia Mound 72 burials, Teouma Lapita intact grave goods). And flag all funerary for sensitivity.\n\n---\n\n## Part 8: Synthesis & Final Recommendation\n\n### Summary of Decision:\n**The F1 Atlas should NOT create independent civilisation deep-dive chapters (F1.14, F1.15, etc.).** Instead:\n\n1. **Keep F1.5–F1.13 as thematic chapters** (current model proven effective)\n2. **Add civilisation context cards to grouping pages** (e.g., \"Nok Grouping,\" \"Chola Grouping,\" \"Iznik Grouping\")\n3. **Create 3–5 civilisation briefs** (750–1500 words) for Nok, Chola, Iznik, Jingdezhen, Benin\n4. **Create 2–3 movement briefs** (1000–2000 words) for Indian Ocean Trade, Waqf Endowments, Horse Domestication/Steppe Mobility\n",
"5. **Link objects bidirectionally** (each object links to its thematic chapter AND its civilisation/movement brief)\n6. **Implement Rules P13–P15** in Historian pack v2 to standardize how civilisation and movement identity are recorded\n\n### Why NOT Separate Deep-Dive Chapters:\n- **Insufficient objects per civilisation:** Nok (1 case), Chola (2), Iznik (2 provisional), Jingdezhen (1)\n- **Thematic organization more effective:** Showing metalworking across Chola, Benin, Luristan, Mediterranean teaches technique diversity better than 5 separate civilisation chapters with only 1 object each\n- **Movements can be taught as networks:** Indian Ocean Trade (F1.13 Networks) naturally connects Chola, Kashan, Jingdezhen across chapters\n- **Already designed for this:** Grouping pages (Nok, Mingei, Igun Eronmwon) show object relationships within a civilisation without requiring a dedicated chapter\n\n### Why INCLUDE Civilisation Context:\n- **Learner need:** Students expect to know \"where did this come from? What civilisation made it?\"\n- **Historical responsibility:** Attributing objects to Nok, Chola, Benin, etc. honors their cultural heritage\n",
"- **Enables comparison:** \"How did Chola metalworking differ from Benin? From Mediterranean?\" requires civilisation-level context\n\n---\n\n## Next Steps\n\n**Immediate (for template v3 & Historian pack v2):**\n1. ✓ Create rules P13–P15 (civilisation, movement, funerary flags)\n2. Create grouping pages for Nok, Chola, Iznik, Jingdezhen, Benin (3–5 exemplar + 2–3 case + 1–2 contextual papers each)\n3. Write 3–5 civilisation context briefs (750–1500 words)\n4. Write 2–3 movement context briefs (1000–2000 words)\n5. Link all objects in register to civilisation_name, movement_name, sensitivity_flag\n\n**Medium-term (for later chapters F1.14+):**\n- If new chapters are added, prioritize movements over civilisations: \"Colonialism & Repatriation\" (F1.24 already covers), \"Living Traditions in the Atlas,\" \"Women Makers & Gender in Craft\"\n- If civilisation chapters are ever added, only add them when 5+ objects per civilisation are available in open-access collections\n\n**Long-term (for Atlas v2):**\n- Reassess object coverage per civilisation; target 3–5 objects per major civilisation (Nok, Chola, Benin, Iznik, Jingdezhen, Egypt, Maya, Indus, China, Islamic World)\n",
"- Add production-context objects (kilns, looms, workshops, tool marks)\n- Add living tradition exemplars (Diné weaving extended; Jamdani expanded; contemporary Nok region craft)\n\n"
],
